"On the Locating Chromatic Number of the Cartesian Product of (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=942459903d5b1d1b7350ac5b9b1f1fef661956bc&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Verifying small cases, encourage us to give the following conjecture. ... Omoomi, On the locating chromatic number of Kneser graphs, to appear in\n4\nSome open problems\nNote that every proper coloring of Km□Kn is equivalent to an m by n Latin rectangle. Moreover, a\nlocating coloring of Km□Kn is equivalent to an m by n Latin rectangle in which, for every two cells\ncontaining the same symbol, there is a symbol that appears only in the row or column of one of them.\nIn what follows we present some open problems related to the obtained results.\nNote that for each given number n, m0 = max{k | k ∈N, k(k −1) −1 ≤n} is a number close\nto √n. If m = m0 ≥4 and (m0(m0 −1) −1) + (m0 −2) ≤n < (m0 + 1)m0 −1, then for each i with\ni < m0, we have\n(n −i2 + 1) −2(i −1) ≥1,\n(n + 1) −i(i −1) −i ≥2m0 −3,\nand n \n 4i−1. Thus, by following the proof of the Theorem 3, we can obtain a locating (n+1)-coloring\nof Km0□Kn and hence, χL(Km0□Kn) = n + 1. Therefore, if m = m0, then the remaining cases for\nn to investigate χL(Km0□Kn) are\nm0(m0 −1) −1 ≤n ≤(m0(m0 −1) −1) + (m0 −3).\nVerifying small cases, encourage us to give the following conjecture.\nConjecture 1. χL(Km0□Kn) = n + 1, where n ≥3 and m0 = max{k | k ∈N, k(k −1) −1 ≤n}.\nBy a long detailed argument, we can prove that χL(Km□Kn) = n + 2 for n\n2 < m ≤⌈n+3\n2 ⌉. For the\nremaining cases we provide the following conjecture.\nConjecture 2. If ⌈n+3\n2 ⌉< m ≤n, then χL(Km□Kn) = n + 3.\nThe similarity of the structures of Km□Cn and Km□Pn is a motivation for the following conjecture.\nConjecture 3. If m and n are suﬃciently large, then χL(Km□Cn) = χL(Km□Pn).\nIt seems that graphs with bigger diameter have smaller locating chromatic number.\nHence, the\nobtained results suggest the following conjecture.\nConjecture 4. For every two connected graphs G and H, χL(G□H) ≤max{ χL(G), χL(H)} + 3.\nReferences\n[1] Asmiati, H. Assiyatun, and E.T. Baskoro, Locating-chromatic number of amalgamation of\nstars, ITB J. Sci. 43, A (2011) 1-8.\n[2] A. Behtoei and B. Omoomi, On the locating chromatic number of Kneser graphs, to appear in\nDiscrete Appl. Math.\n11--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEpidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint (https://www.academia.edu/6216076/Epidemic_Spreading_in_Real_Networks_An_Eigenvalue_Viewpoint)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; description See full PDF download Download PDF ... Epidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint Yang Wang, Deepayan Chakrabarti, Chenxi Wang∗, Christos Faloutsos† Carnegie Mellon University 5000 Forbes Avenue, Pittsburgh, PA, 15213 yangwang, deepayan, chenxi, [email protected] Abstract 1. ... We report a computational study of the spreading&lt;br&gt; dynamics of a viral infection in a complex (scale-free) network. ... For instance, for the spreading of AIDS/HIV, the susceptible individuals can be classified into different cases according to their immunity, and similarly, the infected individuals can be sorted into different classes according to their infectivity. ... In this paper an SIS model for epidemic spreading on semi-directed networks is established, which can be used to examine and compare the impact of undirected and directed contacts on disease spread.\n\n# Epidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint\n\nYang Wang\n\n2003\n\nvisibility\n\n…\n\ndescription\n\n10 pages\n\ndescription See full PDF download Download PDF\n\n## Abstract\n\nHow will a virus propagate in a real network? Does an epidemic threshold exist for a finite powerlaw graph, or any finite graph? How long does it take to disinfect a network given particular values of infection rate and virus death rate?\n\n... Read more\n\n## Key takeaways\n\nImage: sparkles\n\nAI\n\n  1. The proposed model predicts epidemic thresholds using the largest eigenvalue of the adjacency matrix, λ1,A.\n  2. Epidemic thresholds exist for finite graphs and are defined by τ = 1/λ1,A.\n  3. Our model outperforms previous models in predicting virus propagation across arbitrary network topologies.\n  4. Simulation results demonstrate the model's accuracy on real networks, including a 31,180-link dataset.\n  5. Infection rates (β) and curing rates (δ) dictate epidemic outcomes, with exponential decay below the threshold.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEpidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint - researchr publication references (https://researchr.org/publication/WangCWF03/references)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.1 years ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago;   * WangCWF03 ... # PDF ... # Epidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint\n\n# External Links\n\n  * DOI\n  * Google\n  * Google Scholar\n  * MSAS\n\n# Cite Key\n\n  * WangCWF03\n\n# Statistics\n\n  * References: 0\n  * Cited by: 0\n  * Reviews: 0\n  * Bibliographies: 0\n\n# PDF\n\n  * [Upload PDF for personal use]\n\n# Epidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint\n\nYang Wang, Deepayan Chakrabarti, Chenxi Wang, Christos Faloutsos. Epidemic Spreading in Real Networks: An Eigenvalue Viewpoint. In 22nd Symposium on Reliable Distributed Systems (SRDS 2003), 6-8 October 2003, Florence, Italy. pages 25-34, IEEE Computer Society, 2003. [doi]\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Authors\n  * BibTeX\n  * References\n  * Bibliographies\n  * Reviews\n  * Related\n  *   * ## References\n\nNo references recorded for this publication.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCarnegie Mellon University (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5193878d58833b5d216adfb0a3d3387da99b4e73&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1sear